from ../LanguageData import LanguageData
from ../Determinism import Determinism
from ../model/OpenItems import Ambiguity

export anchor WordClasses:
    description:
        A word class, also called a part of speech, is the grammatical job a
        word does: button is a noun, click is a verb, blue is an adjective.
        Knowing the class of each word is what lets analysis find the parts
        of a statement in the next step.

    classes:
        ```text
        noun            names a thing                  button, action, color
        verb            names an action or a state     click, invoke, is
        participle      a verb form used to describe   clicked, dimmed, disabled
        adjective       describes a thing              blue, visible
        adverb          describes a verb or adjective  slightly, never
        determiner      introduces a noun              the, a, every
        pronoun         stands for a named thing       it, its, they
        preposition     relates words by place or role of, to, with, in
        conjunction     joins words or clauses         and, or, but
        condition       opens a condition              when, if, unless
        negation        reverses a statement           not, cannot
        modal           sets how firmly it binds       must, should, can
        number          a number token                 300
        reference       a Piton reference token        @ Button
        unknown         not in the language data
        ```

    evidenceNotTruth:
        A word class says how a word is used in a sentence, not what it means
        in the specification. Disabled is a participle whether it names a
        state of the button or something done to it; deciding which is the
        work of the next chapter, using the specification's own facts. A word
        class is evidence for that decision, never the decision itself.

    howClassesAreFound:
        Many words can belong to more than one class: click is a noun in a
        click and a verb in click the button. So each word starts with every
        class it could have, and rules narrow them down.

        ```text
        1  closed lists     words in the small lists of @{LanguageData}
                            have exactly the class of their list
        2  dictionary       other known words get every class the
                            dictionary lists for them
        3  word endings     unknown words get candidates from their ending:
                            -ly adverb, -ed participle, -ing verb, -s noun or verb
        4  neighbors        rules remove candidates that cannot fit where the
                            word stands, such as a verb straight after the
        ```

        The closed lists and the dictionary are described in @{LanguageData}.
        Every rule that removes a candidate is recorded, so a word's class can
        always be explained by pointing at the list entry, dictionary entry or
        rule that gave it.

    neighborRules:
        ```text
        after a determiner                  not a verb, modal or participle
        after a modal, such as can          a verb, and not a noun
        after a form of be                  a participle or adjective before a noun or verb
        after to                            a verb when a verb is a candidate
        before a noun, after a determiner   an adjective or participle, not a noun
        ```

    whenCandidatesRemain:
        When rules leave more than one candidate, the word keeps all of them.
        The next step tries each, and if they lead to different facts, the
        statement is recorded as an @{Ambiguity}. A word the language data
        does not know at all is classed as unknown, and any statement that
        needs to know its class cannot become a fact.

    noStatisticalTagging:
        Many language tools find word classes with a statistical model that
        picks the most probable class. Analysis does not, because such a model
        cannot explain its choice by pointing at a rule, and a choice that
        cannot be explained cannot be traced. Why this matters, and where
        the boundary lies, is described in @{Determinism}.

    example:
        ```text
        It      pronoun        closed list
        cannot  negation, modal  closed lists
        be      verb           dictionary
        clicked participle     dictionary: clicked is the past participle of click
        .       punctuation
        ```
